<section>
  <heading level="1">9.3 Add and Subtract Square Roots</heading>
  <heading level="2">Learning Objectives</heading>
  <list type="unordered">
    <item>Add and subtract like square roots</item>
    <item>Add and subtract square roots that need simplification</item>
  </list>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Be Prepared" title="Be Prepared" number="9.7">
    <paragraph>Before you get started, take this readiness quiz.</paragraph>
    <paragraph>Add: ⓐ $$3x + 9x$$ ⓑ $$5m + 5n$$ . If you missed this problem, review Example 1.24.</paragraph>
  </note>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Be Prepared" title="Be Prepared" number="9.8">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$\sqrt{50x^{3}}$$ . If you missed this problem, review Example 9.16.</paragraph>
  </note>
  <paragraph>We know that we must follow the order of operations to simplify expressions with square roots. The radical is a grouping symbol, so we work inside the radical first. We simplify $$\sqrt{2 + 7}$$ in this way:</paragraph>
  <paragraph>$$\begin{array}{cccl} &amp; &amp; &amp; \sqrt{2 + 7} \\ \text{Add inside the radical.} &amp; &amp; &amp; \sqrt{9} \\ \text{Simplify.} &amp; &amp; &amp; 3 \end{array}$$</paragraph>
  <paragraph>So if we have to add $$\sqrt{2} + \sqrt{7}$$ , we must not combine them into one radical.</paragraph>
  <paragraph math="block">$$\sqrt{2} + \sqrt{7} \neq \sqrt{2 + 7}$$</paragraph>
  <paragraph>Trying to add square roots with different radicands is like trying to add unlike terms.</paragraph>
  <paragraph>$$\begin{array}{clcl} \text{But, just like we can add} &amp; {x + x,} &amp; \text{we can add} &amp; {\sqrt{3} + \sqrt{3}.} \\ &amp; {x + x = 2x} &amp; &amp; {\sqrt{3} + \sqrt{3} = 2\sqrt{3}} \end{array}$$</paragraph>
  <paragraph>Adding square roots with the same radicand is just like adding like terms. We call square roots with the same radicand like square roots to remind us they work the same as like terms.</paragraph>
  <note type="rule" source_type="Like Square Roots" title="Like Square Roots">
    <paragraph>Square roots with the same radicand are called like square roots.</paragraph>
  </note>
  <paragraph>We add and subtract like square roots in the same way we add and subtract like terms. We know that $$3x + 8x$$ is $$11x$$ . Similarly we add $$3\sqrt{x} + 8\sqrt{x}$$ and the result is $$11\sqrt{x}.$$</paragraph>
  <heading level="2">Add and Subtract Like Square Roots</heading>
  <paragraph>Think about adding like terms with variables as you do the next few examples. When you have like radicands, you just add or subtract the coefficients. When the radicands are not like, you cannot combine the terms.</paragraph>
  <example number="9.29">
    <problem>
      <paragraph>Simplify: $$2\sqrt{2} - 7\sqrt{2}$$ .</paragraph>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell>$$2\sqrt{2} - 7\sqrt{2}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Since the radicals are like, we subtract the coefficients.</cell>
            <cell>$$-5\sqrt{2}$$</cell>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </solution>
  </example>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="9.57">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$8\sqrt{2} - 9\sqrt{2}$$ .</paragraph>
  </note>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="9.58">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$5\sqrt{3} - 9\sqrt{3}$$ .</paragraph>
  </note>
  <example number="9.30">
    <problem>
      <paragraph>Simplify: $$3\sqrt{y} + 4\sqrt{y}$$ .</paragraph>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell>$$3\sqrt{y} + 4\sqrt{y}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Since the radicals are like, we add the coefficients.</cell>
            <cell>$$7\sqrt{y}$$</cell>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </solution>
  </example>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="9.59">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$2\sqrt{x} + 7\sqrt{x}$$ .</paragraph>
  </note>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="9.60">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$5\sqrt{u} + 3\sqrt{u}$$ .</paragraph>
  </note>
  <example number="9.31">
    <problem>
      <paragraph>Simplify: $$4\sqrt{x} - 2\sqrt{y}$$ .</paragraph>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell>$$4\sqrt{x} - 2\sqrt{y}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Since the radicals are not like, we cannotsubtract them. We leave the expression as is.</cell>
            <cell>$$4\sqrt{x} - 2\sqrt{y}$$</cell>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </solution>
  </example>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="9.61">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$7\sqrt{p} - 6\sqrt{q}$$ .</paragraph>
  </note>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="9.62">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$6\sqrt{a} - 3\sqrt{b}$$ .</paragraph>
  </note>
  <example number="9.32">
    <problem>
      <paragraph>Simplify: $$5\sqrt{13} + 4\sqrt{13} + 2\sqrt{13}$$ .</paragraph>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell>$$5\sqrt{13} + 4\sqrt{13} + 2\sqrt{13}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Since the radicals are like, we add the coefficients.</cell>
            <cell>$$11\sqrt{13}$$</cell>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </solution>
  </example>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="9.63">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$4\sqrt{11} + 2\sqrt{11} + 3\sqrt{11}$$ .</paragraph>
  </note>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="9.64">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$6\sqrt{10} + 2\sqrt{10} + 3\sqrt{10}$$ .</paragraph>
  </note>
  <example number="9.33">
    <problem>
      <paragraph>Simplify: $$2\sqrt{6} - 6\sqrt{6} + 3\sqrt{3}$$ .</paragraph>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell>$$2\sqrt{6} - 6\sqrt{6} + 3\sqrt{3}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Since the first two radicals are like, wesubtract their coefficients.</cell>
            <cell>$$- 4\sqrt{6} + 3\sqrt{3}$$</cell>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </solution>
  </example>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="9.65">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$5\sqrt{5} - 4\sqrt{5} + 2\sqrt{6}$$ .</paragraph>
  </note>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="9.66">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$3\sqrt{7} - 8\sqrt{7} + 2\sqrt{5}$$ .</paragraph>
  </note>
  <example number="9.34">
    <problem>
      <paragraph>Simplify: $$2\sqrt{5n} - 6\sqrt{5n} + 4\sqrt{5n}$$ .</paragraph>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell>$$2\sqrt{5n} - 6\sqrt{5n} + 4\sqrt{5n}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Since the radicals are like, we combine them.</cell>
            <cell>$$0\sqrt{5n}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Simplify.</cell>
            <cell>0</cell>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </solution>
  </example>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="9.67">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$\sqrt{7x} - 7\sqrt{7x} + 4\sqrt{7x}$$ .</paragraph>
  </note>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="9.68">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$4\sqrt{3y} - 7\sqrt{3y} + 2\sqrt{3y}$$ .</paragraph>
  </note>
  <paragraph>When radicals contain more than one variable, as long as all the variables and their exponents are identical, the radicals are like.</paragraph>
  <example number="9.35">
    <problem>
      <paragraph>Simplify: $$\sqrt{3xy} + 5\sqrt{3xy} - 4\sqrt{3xy}$$ .</paragraph>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell>$$\sqrt{3xy} + 5\sqrt{3xy} - 4\sqrt{3xy}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Since the radicals are like, we combine them.</cell>
            <cell>$$2\sqrt{3xy}$$</cell>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </solution>
  </example>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="9.69">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$\sqrt{5xy} + 4\sqrt{5xy} - 7\sqrt{5xy}$$ .</paragraph>
  </note>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="9.70">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$3\sqrt{7mn} + \sqrt{7mn} - 4\sqrt{7mn}$$ .</paragraph>
  </note>
  <heading level="2">Add and Subtract Square Roots that Need Simplification</heading>
  <paragraph>Remember that we always simplify square roots by removing the largest perfect-square factor. Sometimes when we have to add or subtract square roots that do not appear to have like radicals, we find like radicals after simplifying the square roots.</paragraph>
  <example number="9.36">
    <problem>
      <paragraph>Simplify: $$\sqrt{20} + 3\sqrt{5}$$ .</paragraph>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell>$$\sqrt{20} + 3\sqrt{5}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Simplify the radicals, when possible.</cell>
            <cell>$$\sqrt{4} \cdot \sqrt{5} + 3\sqrt{5}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell>$$2\sqrt{5} + 3\sqrt{5}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Combine the like radicals.</cell>
            <cell>$$5\sqrt{5}$$</cell>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </solution>
  </example>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="9.71">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$\sqrt{18} + 6\sqrt{2}$$ .</paragraph>
  </note>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="9.72">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$\sqrt{27} + 4\sqrt{3}$$ .</paragraph>
  </note>
  <example number="9.37">
    <problem>
      <paragraph>Simplify: $$\sqrt{48} - \sqrt{75}$$ .</paragraph>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell>$$\sqrt{48} - \sqrt{75}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Simplify the radicals.</cell>
            <cell>$$\sqrt{16} \cdot \sqrt{3} - \sqrt{25} \cdot \sqrt{3}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell>$$4\sqrt{3} - 5\sqrt{3}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Combine the like radicals.</cell>
            <cell>$$-\sqrt{3}$$</cell>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </solution>
  </example>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="9.73">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$\sqrt{32} - \sqrt{18}$$ .</paragraph>
  </note>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="9.74">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$\sqrt{20} - \sqrt{45}$$ .</paragraph>
  </note>
  <paragraph>Just like we use the Associative Property of Multiplication to simplify $$5\left( {3x} \right)$$ and get $$15x$$ , we can simplify $$5\left( {3\sqrt{x}} \right)$$ and get $$15\sqrt{x}$$ . We will use the Associative Property to do this in the next example.</paragraph>
  <example number="9.38">
    <problem>
      <paragraph>Simplify: $$5\sqrt{18} - 2\sqrt{8}$$ .</paragraph>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell>$$5\sqrt{18} - 2\sqrt{8}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Simplify the radicals.</cell>
            <cell>$$5 \cdot \sqrt{9} \cdot \sqrt{2} - 2 \cdot \sqrt{4} \cdot \sqrt{2}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell>$$5 \cdot 3 \cdot \sqrt{2} - 2 \cdot 2 \cdot \sqrt{2}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell>$$15\sqrt{2} - 4\sqrt{2}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Combine the like radicals.</cell>
            <cell>$$11\sqrt{2}$$</cell>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </solution>
  </example>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="9.75">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$4\sqrt{27} - 3\sqrt{12}$$ .</paragraph>
  </note>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="9.76">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$3\sqrt{20} - 7\sqrt{45}$$ .</paragraph>
  </note>
  <example number="9.39">
    <problem>
      <paragraph>Simplify: $$\frac{3}{4}\sqrt{192} - \frac{5}{6}\sqrt{108}$$ .</paragraph>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell>$$\frac{3}{4}\sqrt{192} - \frac{5}{6}\sqrt{108}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Simplify the radicals.</cell>
            <cell>$$\frac{3}{4}\sqrt{64} \cdot \sqrt{3} - \frac{5}{6}\sqrt{36} \cdot \sqrt{3}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell>$$\frac{3}{4} \cdot 8 \cdot \sqrt{3} - \frac{5}{6} \cdot 6 \cdot \sqrt{3}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell>$$6\sqrt{3} - 5\sqrt{3}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Combine the like radicals.</cell>
            <cell>$$\sqrt{3}$$</cell>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </solution>
  </example>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="9.77">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$\frac{2}{3}\sqrt{108} - \frac{5}{7}\sqrt{147}$$ .</paragraph>
  </note>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="9.78">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$\frac{3}{5}\sqrt{200} - \frac{3}{4}\sqrt{128}$$ .</paragraph>
  </note>
  <example number="9.40">
    <problem>
      <paragraph>Simplify: $$\frac{2}{3}\sqrt{48} - \frac{3}{4}\sqrt{12}$$ .</paragraph>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell>$$\frac{2}{3}\sqrt{48} - \frac{3}{4}\sqrt{12}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Simplify the radicals.</cell>
            <cell>$$\frac{2}{3}\sqrt{16} \cdot \sqrt{3} - \frac{3}{4}\sqrt{4} \cdot \sqrt{3}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell>$$\frac{2}{3} \cdot 4 \cdot \sqrt{3} - \frac{3}{4} \cdot 2 \cdot \sqrt{3}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell>$$\frac{8}{3}\sqrt{3} - \frac{3}{2}\sqrt{3}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Find a common denominator to subtract thecoefficients of the like radicals.</cell>
            <cell>$$\frac{16}{6}\sqrt{3} - \frac{9}{6}\sqrt{3}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Simplify.</cell>
            <cell>$$\frac{7}{6}\sqrt{3}$$</cell>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </solution>
  </example>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="9.79">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$\frac{2}{5}\sqrt{32} - \frac{1}{3}\sqrt{8}$$ .</paragraph>
  </note>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="9.80">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$\frac{1}{3}\sqrt{80} - \frac{1}{4}\sqrt{125}$$ .</paragraph>
  </note>
  <paragraph>In the next example, we will remove constant and variable factors from the square roots.</paragraph>
  <example number="9.41">
    <problem>
      <paragraph>Simplify: $$\sqrt{18n^{5}} - \sqrt{32n^{5}}$$ .</paragraph>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell>$$\sqrt{18n^{5}} - \sqrt{32n^{5}}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Simplify the radicals.</cell>
            <cell>$$\sqrt{9n^{4}} \cdot \sqrt{2n} - \sqrt{16n^{4}} \cdot \sqrt{2n}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell>$$3n^{2}\sqrt{2n} - 4n^{2}\sqrt{2n}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Combine the like radicals.</cell>
            <cell>$$-n^{2}\sqrt{2n}$$</cell>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </solution>
  </example>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="9.81">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$\sqrt{32m^{7}} - \sqrt{50m^{7}}$$ .</paragraph>
  </note>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="9.82">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$\sqrt{27p^{3}} - \sqrt{48p^{3}}$$ .</paragraph>
  </note>
  <example number="9.42">
    <problem>
      <paragraph>Simplify: $$9\sqrt{50m^{2}} - 6\sqrt{48m^{2}}$$ .</paragraph>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell>$$9\sqrt{50m^{2}} - 6\sqrt{48m^{2}}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Simplify the radicals.</cell>
            <cell>$$9\sqrt{25m^{2}} \cdot \sqrt{2} - 6\sqrt{16m^{2}} \cdot \sqrt{3}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell>$$9 \cdot 5m \cdot \sqrt{2} - 6 \cdot 4m \cdot \sqrt{3}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell>$$45m\sqrt{2} - 24m\sqrt{3}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>The radicals are not like and so cannot be combined.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </solution>
  </example>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="9.83">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$5\sqrt{32x^{2}} - 3\sqrt{48x^{2}}$$ .</paragraph>
  </note>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="9.84">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$7\sqrt{48y^{2}} - 4\sqrt{72y^{2}}$$ .</paragraph>
  </note>
  <example number="9.43">
    <problem>
      <paragraph>Simplify: $$2\sqrt{8x^{2}} - 5x\sqrt{32} + 5\sqrt{18x^{2}}$$ .</paragraph>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell>$$2\sqrt{8x^{2}} - 5x\sqrt{32} + 5\sqrt{18x^{2}}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Simplify the radicals.</cell>
            <cell>$$2\sqrt{4x^{2}} \cdot \sqrt{2} - 5x\sqrt{16} \cdot \sqrt{2} + 5\sqrt{9x^{2}} \cdot \sqrt{2}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell>$$2 \cdot 2x \cdot \sqrt{2} - 5x \cdot 4 \cdot \sqrt{2} + 5 \cdot 3x \cdot \sqrt{2}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell>$$4x\sqrt{2} - 20x\sqrt{2} + 15x\sqrt{2}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Combine the like radicals.</cell>
            <cell>$$-x\sqrt{2}$$</cell>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </solution>
  </example>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="9.85">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$3\sqrt{12x^{2}} - 2x\sqrt{48} + 4\sqrt{27x^{2}}$$ .</paragraph>
  </note>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="9.86">
    <paragraph>Simplify: $$3\sqrt{18x^{2}} - 6x\sqrt{32} + 2\sqrt{50x^{2}}$$ .</paragraph>
  </note>
